## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects, arrays, and functions compare by **reference**.

Every render creates new references for inline objects/functions, so a child receiving them always sees "new" props — even if the values are identical.

```jsx
// Bad: new object reference on every parent render
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// Bad: new function reference on every parent render
<Child onClick={() => handleClick(id)} />
```

---

### 2. The Three Memoization Tools

| Tool | Memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | A component's output | Child re-renders are expensive and props are stable |
| `useMemo` | A computed value | Expensive calculation; value passed as prop or used in dep array |
| `useCallback` | A function reference | Function passed as prop to a memoized child |

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props haven't changed (shallow compare).

```jsx
const Chart = React.memo(({ data }) => <ExpensiveChart data={data} />);
```

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a value across renders.

```jsx
const sorted = useMemo(() => data.sort(compareFn), [data]);
```

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function identity.

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

> **Rule of thumb:** `useCallback(fn, deps)` is just `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`. Don't reach for either unless there's a measurable problem — memoization has its own cost.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Two common traps:

**Pitfall 1 — Inline value object:** Every parent render creates a new object, triggering all consumers.

```jsx
// Bad
<AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, logout }}>

// Good: memoize the value
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, logout }), [user, logout]);
<AuthContext.Provider value={value}>
```

**Pitfall 2 — Coarse context:** One large context means all consumers re-render whenever *any* field changes. Split contexts by update frequency (e.g., `UserContext` vs. `ThemeContext`).

---

### 4. Before / After Example

**Before** — `List` re-renders every time `Parent` renders, even when `items` hasn't changed, because `onSelect` is a new function each time.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <List
        items={['a', 'b', 'c']}
        onSelect={(item) => setSelected(item)}  // new ref every render
      />
    </>
  );
}

const List = ({ items, onSelect }) => {
  console.log('List rendered'); // fires on every count increment
  return items.map(i => <div key={i} onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
};
```

**After** — `List` only re-renders when `items` or `onSelect` actually changes.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => {
    setSelected(item);
  }, []); // stable reference — no deps change

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <List items={['a', 'b', 'c']} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const List = React.memo(({ items, onSelect }) => {
  console.log('List rendered'); // only fires when items/onSelect change
  return items.map(i => <div key={i} onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
});
```

**The fix is the combination:** `React.memo` makes the component opt-in to skipping, and `useCallback` ensures the prop reference is actually stable. Either alone is insufficient — `memo` without `useCallback` still sees a new `onSelect` every time.

---

**When NOT to optimize:** Memoization adds complexity and its own overhead. Profile first with React DevTools Profiler. Optimize components that render frequently with expensive subtrees, not everything.
